Don't crash GET_STATUS on a core whose .info has no systemid.

command_get_status() copies core_info->system_id into the reply with
strlcpy(), and system_id is NULL when the core's .info file has no
'systemid' line. strlcpy() faults on it and RetroArch dies with SIGSEGV
on the network command. On MLP1 that is any in-game Menu tap: jawakad
sends GET_STATUS before it shows the in-game menu, so the tap kills the
game instead. Verified with Game & Watch (gw_libretro.info) and EasyRPG
(easyrpg_libretro.info), both pc 0x1f0908 / lr 0x4a7c8 in Leaf's v1.22.2
MLP1 build.

Fall back to the core's library_name, and to an empty field when that
is missing too. The reply keeps its shape ("GET_STATUS PLAYING
<system>,<content>,crc32=..."), so jawakad's parser is unaffected. Upstream
fixed the same dereference after v1.22.2 in 68e8274a31
(libretro/RetroArch#18585), with "UNKNOWN" as the last resort; drop
this patch once Leaf builds a RetroArch that has it.

diff --git a/command.c b/command.c
index 21e6878c8c..f1b8c9ccbb 100644
--- a/command.c
+++ b/command.c
@@ -1660,12 +1660,16 @@ bool command_get_status(command_t *cmd, const char* arg)
       else
          _len += strlcpy(reply + _len, "PLAYING", sizeof(reply) - _len);
       _len    += strlcpy(reply + _len, " ", sizeof(reply) - _len);
-      if (core_info)
-         _len += strlcpy(reply + _len, core_info->system_id,
-               sizeof(reply) - _len);
-      else
-         _len += strlcpy(reply + _len, runloop_st->system.info.library_name,
-               sizeof(reply) - _len);
+      {
+         /* system_id is NULL for a core whose .info has no systemid */
+         const char *system_id = NULL;
+         if (core_info && !string_is_empty(core_info->system_id))
+            system_id = core_info->system_id;
+         else if (!string_is_empty(runloop_st->system.info.library_name))
+            system_id = runloop_st->system.info.library_name;
+         if (system_id)
+            _len += strlcpy(reply + _len, system_id, sizeof(reply) - _len);
+      }
       _len    += strlcpy(reply + _len, ",", sizeof(reply) - _len);
       _len    += strlcpy(reply + _len,
             path_basename(path_get(RARCH_PATH_BASENAME)), sizeof(reply) - _len);
